module gunbc.scm.commit_closure_json_v2

// THE MODULE NAME CARRIES A VERSION AND THE DOCUMENT CARRIES A DIFFERENT ONE. `_v2` named the second
// CODEC GENERATION -- the predecessor that encoded the root positionally, since deleted -- so with
// that sibling gone the suffix distinguishes nothing, and once the schema grew a member and the tag
// became `…-v3`, this module announces v2 in its name and v3 in its bytes. THE TAG IS THE CONTRACT
// AND THE NAME IS A FOSSIL: `closure_document_format_tag` is the value the encoder emits and the
// decoder compares, and it is the only thing that decides which schema this build writes.
//
// THE RENAME IS DEFERRED, AND THE REASON IS AN EXTERNALITY RATHER THAN EFFORT. It was authored and
// reverted after measuring what it costs. A module rename re-targets every binding that names it,
// which the required namespace-wave-admission phase classifies `TargetChanged` -- not auto-admitted,
// so each needs a row in NAMESPACE_TRANSITION_ADMISSIONS. This rename produced 41. That roster's own
// header records what happened the last time rows sat on main: `stale_admissions` is computed PER
// RUN, so rows on main are inherited by every open pull request, and a change that touches no
// namespace can never match them -- the phase then refuses every unrelated lane until the rows are
// dissolved. Landing 41 of them to fix a name would spend every other lane's CI to buy this one's
// tidiness, which is the externalized cost DESIGN section 5 names, and it would do it inside a pull
// request whose subject is the object model.
//
// So the fork is REPORTED rather than carried silently, and it is not repaired by this comment
// existing: a reader who trusts the module path over the emitted tag is still wrong. What this note
// buys is that the two are not equally authoritative-looking. dissolve-on: a rename landed as its
// own change, with its admission rows authored and deleted in the same wave, at a moment when the
// open-PR population can absorb them -- not folded into an unrelated subject.

